Bryon Mackay: Alright, who here is vibe-coded? I don't know if y'all have access to emojis, but I'm gonna assume all of you are raising your hand. We've all vibe-coded, right? It's a fun time. Wow, it makes programming so fun and so fast. You can just, with literally anything you can dream, you can see it.

46
00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:11.800
Bryon Mackay: And it's amazing.

47
00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:24.949
Bryon Mackay: So, there's… there's problems with that, right? Right? I… I have Vibecoded an app with my son, and I'm afraid to look at the code, right? I… I don't even want to know what's under… what's underneath that blanket. So we'll…

48
00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:32.560
Bryon Mackay: But anyway, so how do we fix this? So we can't do this in our jobs. We're engineers. We've got to make sure things are quality, that things are good.

49
00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:47.529
Bryon Mackay: Here's some, interesting stats that I think will, highlight just how prevalent these issues are that we're seeing. Ai-generated PRs contain 1.7 times more issues overall than if a human did it.

50
00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:51.899
Bryon Mackay: So, we're getting more issues. Are they sneaking through? You bet they are.

51
00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:59.479
Bryon Mackay: So, this isn't a reason to stop AI, this is a reason for us to stop and consider what we're doing with AI.

52
00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:12.730
Bryon Mackay: You know, I thought about this the other… last night as I was preparing for this. If somebody came up to you 10 years ago and said, here is somebody who has 1.7… who makes errors 1.7 more times than any of your engineers.

53
00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:22.840
Bryon Mackay: Would you hire this person? You'd be like, no. But then you'd say, what if they could code 100 times faster? You'd be like, well, maybe, you know? So it's this funny, like, what kind of world we're living in.

54
00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.280
Bryon Mackay: But yeah, a lot more issues. Here's some more issues.

55
00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:36.350
Bryon Mackay: Performance regressions are 7x more likely. Readability is 3X, has 3X more issues. Security vulnerabilities, that's a big one. 2.74.

56
00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:54.930
Bryon Mackay: I mean, we're talking… these are some serious numbers here of more issues that could be coming out, and we don't even know, what, you know, what's going on. Like, we don't even know why, initially, because we probably didn't read the, you know, I'm not saying all of you, but I'm imagining a lot of us probably didn't read the code thoroughly.

57
00:08:55.350 --> 00:09:06.799
Bryon Mackay: Error handling gaps, two times more common. Logic and correctness, 75% more common. Okay, this is the kind of stuff that's getting out there.

58
00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:21.729
Bryon Mackay: Okay? This is the kind of issues that we're seeing. AI and LLMs are not doing a better quality job. They're just doing it a whole lot faster. And that's what we're real… that's what we've been focused on for so long, right? 10x engineer!

59
00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.639
Bryon Mackay: Right? We hear that all the time.

60
00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:29.879
Bryon Mackay: What does that really mean? It's just, they're just coding faster, but they're actually coding worse.

61
00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:31.490
Bryon Mackay: So…

62
00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:37.069
Bryon Mackay: This is where we come in and go, okay, let's figure out what's going on. What can we do about this?

63
00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:46.719
Bryon Mackay: All right. The first point here is, I think, pretty, pretty evident to all of… to any of us who's tried to do this, especially at a production level.

64
00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:51.979
Bryon Mackay: Right? We need to make sure there's a lot of context. So…

65
00:09:52.200 --> 00:10:05.539
Bryon Mackay: They have no knowledge of your business rules, nowhere of architectural constraints. Sure, Cloud Code will go through your codebase and try to understand all these things. It will do its very best, and quite frankly, it does a really impressive job at that.

66
00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:24.830
Bryon Mackay: But it's still missing constraints. It still doesn't know about this function that's buried over here that could actually be really helpful in this situation, right? There's a lot of things like that that we as engineers for years and years and years prided ourselves on… ourselves on, on knowing, because we wrote the code by hand, and so we knew all the ins and outs.

67
00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:37.289
Bryon Mackay: No understanding configuration patterns. This last one, is something that I want to, highlight here, and that is that a lot of times, and I do this, I think we all probably do this to some extent.

68
00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:50.760
Bryon Mackay: we will keep a Claude session open, or a codec session open for so long that we are just… we just have exceeded the reasonable level of context in the context window.

69
00:10:51.010 --> 00:10:54.690
Bryon Mackay: So, context window currently is, what, a million for Opus, right?

70
00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:58.459
Bryon Mackay: Okay, a million does not mean we should use a million tokens.

71
00:10:58.580 --> 00:10:59.470
Bryon Mackay: Right?

72
00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.410
Bryon Mackay: I would stop at $100,000.

73
00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:16.840
Bryon Mackay: If we get to 100,000, stop, consolidate, do something, you know, start a new session, whatever it takes. But by that point, there are studies that are showing that it is starting to lose its ability to remember things at that point.

74
00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:27.009
Bryon Mackay: Okay, so that's something that we have to be, really cognizant of as well, is how much context are we using? And I'm guilty of this. I have context windows that are…

75
00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:41.459
Bryon Mackay: super bloated that I just want to keep going back to, because it went so well last time, but it inevitably, will cause more… well, not to say more issues, but it'll just… yeah, it will be more issues. It'll come at a more… at a more frequent clip, right?

76
00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:48.460
Bryon Mackay: And so, that's something we have to be, really keen on, really aware of, as we… as we work through our code.

77
00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.110
Bryon Mackay: But here's the bigger thing.

78
00:11:54.440 --> 00:12:06.200
Bryon Mackay: We do not have the time to review all the code that an AI produces. Now, I want to challenge you here for a second, because every company will say at some point.

79
00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:12.940
Bryon Mackay: We want more code. Look at these things, they go so fast. 10x engineer, yadda yadda yadda yadda, right?

80
00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:15.479
Bryon Mackay: What if we said no?

81
00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:33.920
Bryon Mackay: What if we said we're not doing that? What if we said we're actually going to go 2X faster, or something to that extent, right? Before AI, issues arrived at human speed, we could review them, right? You had your engineer sitting next to you, you'd write code, he'd write code, halfway through the week, you'd swap code and read each other's code.

82
00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:36.450
Bryon Mackay: You know, that was manageable, right?

83
00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:49.559
Bryon Mackay: Now, things are coming out so fast, oh, you can, you can, you know, essentially vibecode your way through a problem, and it's, like, you just keep pushing out features. I had…

84
00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:58.829
Bryon Mackay: I had a situation where I had a junior engineer sitting next to me, and I asked them to go do some task. And he came back with a 300-line PR.

85
00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:05.270
Bryon Mackay: And I looked at the PR, and I mean, about 20 lines in, I go, this could all be done in about 3 lines.

86
00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:07.799
Bryon Mackay: And so I went back to him and was like, hey.

87
00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:14.329
Bryon Mackay: you've… here's your solution. I'm just gonna… I'm just gonna give it to you and tell you what it is, because what you have is way too much code.

88
00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:25.260
Bryon Mackay: It's 3 lines, to do something that he had, essentially had an AI bill 300 for. So there's these checks, right, that we need to be aware of. I'll say this again later.

89
00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:26.340
Bryon Mackay: But…

90
00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:36.879
Bryon Mackay: the bottleneck that we should experience, we all… you know, what is the bottleneck? Well, it's reviews, right? No. The bottleneck is our ability to ensure quality.

91
00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:39.400
Bryon Mackay: And however that happens, happens.

92
00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:41.770
Bryon Mackay: But that should be the bottleneck.

93
00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:56.909
Bryon Mackay: Right? So, we have to be really, leery, and even push back when we're being pushed to do things to our… and that might be a hard thing for some leaders to hear, but as engineers, it needs to start with us.

94
00:13:57.490 --> 00:14:02.009
Bryon Mackay: So… Alright, so what do we do here?

95
00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:12.739
Bryon Mackay: Where do we start? So let's say we want to make sure that we talked about context, we've talked about quality, right? And assuring quality. So where do we start first?

96
00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:27.969
Bryon Mackay: Pseudocoding the logic first is a great way to go. Doesn't have to be pseudocode in your codebase, but it could be something where you are, where you talk it out, where you really think deeply about how is this going to work. I really find it valuable

97
00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:42.129
Bryon Mackay: To tell… to build out documents that… where I, like, go through the logic myself, and I go, this is how I expect it to be built. Like, that's kind of my practice, too, with a lot of PRs, like, I expect it to be built this way, let's see how they end up doing it.

98
00:14:42.360 --> 00:15:00.609
Bryon Mackay: But you provide that kind of guidance to the LLM, and suddenly it knows exactly where it should go, right? Us as engineers need to be aware of our codebases. We are aware of our codebases, and so there's a lot of guidance that we should be providing, as opposed to saying, hey, go make this button, and make it

99
00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:03.639
Bryon Mackay: You know, make this button so they can change their status.

100
00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:16.140
Bryon Mackay: go through the steps a little bit more. How do they… how does this button, you know, how do you want this button to send the API request? How do you want that API request to get to your service layer? How do you want that to go to the database? Should you make…

101
00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:27.890
Bryon Mackay: And just be a lot more, a lot more detailed in your context that you provide. Don't let the LLM decide, because heaven knows, as we've seen in those numbers, it will do a poor job.

102
00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:32.890
Bryon Mackay: Of course, this goes back to research,

103
00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:49.479
Bryon Mackay: And I'll go into this a little bit. I do have a practice that I really like doing when it comes to doing research. And it's not really research as much as it is having a team architecture conversation. So what we'll do is we will get the engineers in a room, the engineers that are working on said task.

104
00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:52.749
Bryon Mackay: we'll turn on Notion. Notion is my…

105
00:15:52.950 --> 00:16:02.489
Bryon Mackay: transcriber of choice, personally. I just… I like it. There's lots of great transcribers, but that's the one I like to use. Turn on Notion, and we'll just start talking for an hour or two.

106
00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:12.559
Bryon Mackay: About whatever the, whatever the, you know, task is at hand. We'll go into a lot of detail, like I was talking about. We'll go into a lot of detail of how to get

107
00:16:12.670 --> 00:16:27.620
Bryon Mackay: this task completed, and the changes that need to go into making it. Not saying to do this for everyone. Of course, there are small tasks that you can get by with without this much rigor, but for larger tasks, it's important for us on the team

108
00:16:27.770 --> 00:16:41.499
Bryon Mackay: to know all the innards of what's going on, right? I don't want to be an engineer on the island. I want to make sure my team's aware, and we have these conversations, so that we can build this context up.

109
00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:55.799
Bryon Mackay: When we're done with our conversation, and it's all transcribed in Notion, we go through the transcription… not the transcription, it usually… we then take that and say to Claude, hey, go take this transcription and build out, a spec list.

110
00:16:56.080 --> 00:17:00.469
Bryon Mackay: And filled out some tasks. And then we go through those meticulously.

111
00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:18.360
Bryon Mackay: And make sure that those align with what we're doing, and then we can go to the AI. Once we have those edited, fleshed out, and we're feeling really good about where we are, then we go to, you know, COLD Code, or Codex, or whatever you're using, and implement it. But it still just takes a lot of thought, and a lot of engineering know-how.

112
00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:31.130
Bryon Mackay: To get there. So, when I say pseudocode logic first, there's also a lot of planning involved. Of course, there's research involved if there's things you don't know. But there's this collaboration between engineers, too, that goes a long, long way.

113
00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:34.330
Bryon Mackay: Okay.

114
00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:39.310
Bryon Mackay: There's a couple other things you can do, and this is more on a team level.

115
00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:53.950
Bryon Mackay: In terms of what you can accomplish. So, context files are super helpful, especially when you have teams, when you have a repo that you have many, many people working on, and they all have different,

116
00:17:54.140 --> 00:18:00.079
Bryon Mackay: I'd say mastery, understanding of different areas of it, of your codebase.

117
00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:18.439
Bryon Mackay: So you have these CloudMD files. Codex has one as well, I think they're called Agents.md, I can't remember the exact name. But you basically can put these to set rules and constraints and patterns and understandings so that you're not relying on the LLM to go through your codebase and understand what's going on every single time.

118
00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:24.549
Bryon Mackay: Instead, it will rely on these files to go in and understand the rules and the constraints of a given file.

119
00:18:24.970 --> 00:18:41.870
Bryon Mackay: A good example of this would be, to, to say, don't, you know, you're not allowed… you're only allowed to change authentication logic in this one spot. You cannot put authentication logic anywhere else in the codebase, it has to go through this file.

120
00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:56.939
Bryon Mackay: Right? That will stop the LLM from going out and trying to do some things that are just not safe, or try to bypass some security measures that you've already established. So those are the kinds of things that you want to put inside those context files to give that LLM, that coding agent.

121
00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:05.979
Bryon Mackay: It's, it's guidance so that it does not go off the rails and start to cause those security issues or other performance issues that we were talking about earlier.

122
00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:07.650
Bryon Mackay: Okay?

123
00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:26.140
Bryon Mackay: Centralizing risk, something I just kind of mentioned in that example, but making sure there's, a single owner, mainly like the files, right? Everything's consolidated, it's in one spot, and anytime those change, that should raise a flag, right? You can set these triggers here and there, to say.

124
00:19:26.140 --> 00:19:33.820
Bryon Mackay: You know, when this file changes, we need to get a review from so-and-so, or we need to get a review from this guy, or that guy, or from platform, or from security.

125
00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:47.629
Bryon Mackay: You know, those things are all really, really good processes to have in place to make sure that the code that we're writing and the changes that we're making, even if it's a junior to the most senior, they're all getting reviewed by the same people. We all know what's going on.

126
00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:48.560
Bryon Mackay: Right?

127
00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:58.540
Bryon Mackay: Smaller PRs. Now, there's always give and take when there's a number involved here, especially something like lines of code, but…

128
00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:06.649
Bryon Mackay: It's more of a code smell, I'll admit, but if you're getting around the 400 line mark, or getting above it, especially significantly.

129
00:20:06.690 --> 00:20:22.589
Bryon Mackay: Think about it. Think about it for a second. Why is it taking so much code? More likely than not these days, the coding agent is bloating, right? They're usually going out there and they're just writing a whole lot of code to get something done, and it's a good time to stop and be like, wait a minute, what's really going on here?

130
00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:27.550
Bryon Mackay: Your PR reviewer will thank you too, as you can probably imagine.

131
00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:33.720
Bryon Mackay: The 7 file change, again, it's not really a hard number, it's a bit arbitrary.

132
00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:34.820
Bryon Mackay: But…

133
00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:53.259
Bryon Mackay: It's a… it's kind of that rule of, like a code smell. Again, if you're changing 15 files, you're going, wait, wait, why are we doing so many file changes? Now, sometimes you have to, right? Sometimes there are cases where you're actually just, you know… but you know what those are. I'm not trying to… I'm not trying to simplify… oversimplify this a bit.

134
00:20:53.260 --> 00:21:00.110
Bryon Mackay: But again, it's just good things to keep in mind. Like, LLMs are great at writing a whole lot of code.

135
00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:11.680
Bryon Mackay: And this is just one of those opportunities where you can sit there and go, when I see a PR with, like, a thousand line changes, I'm gonna stop and go, wait, what's really going on here? Why do we need all of these line changes? Let me understand this better.

136
00:21:13.010 --> 00:21:31.040
Bryon Mackay: Another thing that I've seen a lot in the code from our challengers, which is actually quite surprised me, is naming. There gets to be a lot of generic names that LLMs will throw into code, and that can be problematic when you try to read it.

137
00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:37.520
Bryon Mackay: Generic names, or using, you know, small shorthand.

138
00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:45.449
Bryon Mackay: There's just… there's just no room for that. And the way that we can remedy is quite simple, just tell… either… either you name it yourself.

139
00:21:45.800 --> 00:22:00.039
Bryon Mackay: Or just remind the LLM to be explicit and see how that goes, but I would lean into you naming it yourself when it's really critical, and when it's important, so that you can make sure that when you come back and read it, you know what's going on.

140
00:22:00.500 --> 00:22:01.420
Bryon Mackay: Right?

141
00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:10.460
Bryon Mackay: Finally, checkpoint commits, right? Commit at logical checkpoints, these are all things that are really useful.

142
00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:17.560
Bryon Mackay: And one tool that I… I'm not affiliated with them, but I'm just gonna throw it out there. If anyone has used Graphite.

143
00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:21.799
Bryon Mackay: Graphite is an excellent tool for this kind of thing.

144
00:22:22.390 --> 00:22:31.949
Bryon Mackay: Reason being is because what they… I'll just really quickly touch on it. It can sound a little confusing if you're not used to this kind of thing, but basically they'll make a stack of PRs.

145
00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:39.830
Bryon Mackay: Where every PR relies on the previous PR, right? It's built on top of itself. But they have some really great functionality around

146
00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:54.210
Bryon Mackay: If you have to make a change at, like, the bottom of the stack, that stack, that then gets propagated to the rest of the stack. Really, really helpful, when it comes to, when it comes to, like, building small PRs and building them in

147
00:22:54.210 --> 00:23:07.259
Bryon Mackay: And kind of some sort of scope, where it's like, hey, these are going to be my database changes, then it's gonna be my service layer, then it's gonna be my API layer. Really, really helpful, when it comes to putting all those together, so…

148
00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:16.259
Bryon Mackay: If you haven't looked into it, I recommend it. It's not for everybody, and it's not for every use case, but I think graphite and the process they have is, super helpful, so…

149
00:23:18.410 --> 00:23:19.390
Bryon Mackay: Okay.

150
00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:26.880
Bryon Mackay: This… this is another thing that I've seen so much of, and I'm totally guilty of this, and I'm learning better ways now.

151
00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:35.519
Bryon Mackay: It is really easy for LLMs to make tests, and we all feel so good about it, because we're like, look.

152
00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:46.249
Bryon Mackay: I'm running tests, and this might be the first time in years that I've really written this many tests. But the fact of the matter is, is that these tests are usually low value.

153
00:23:46.250 --> 00:24:00.700
Bryon Mackay: My favorite one is when they're verifying that a static map is indeed that static map, and it's like, no, we don't need to test that, like, this is a string, we don't need to spec that this value is here, because

154
00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:14.529
Bryon Mackay: That's just what it is. So, there's a lot of tests that it'll write that are low value. Now, I'm not saying there's no value in them, okay? There's value in them, and we should. We should be having them write tests.

155
00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:24.419
Bryon Mackay: But there's an opportunity here for us to consider what other tests we might want this, to verify that this is actually working.

156
00:24:24.460 --> 00:24:42.979
Bryon Mackay: And I'll give you a couple examples of categories. We've got edge cases, right? There's a lot of edge cases we can test. Security is another one of those areas where we can make sure that, yes, a test for this security thing is actually there. One thing that you have to be a little more mindful of sometimes with coding agents.

157
00:24:43.690 --> 00:24:54.080
Bryon Mackay: is verifying parameters, right? So, sometimes I've seen coding agents where they'll automatically do it, other times they do not, and they really should.

158
00:24:54.660 --> 00:25:10.150
Bryon Mackay: And then those are things, of course, that you would test. So, there's… I would almost… I would… I like to think that thinking through the tests are just as important, and this is a little… this has been said for many, many, many years, especially if you're in a TDD environment.

159
00:25:10.490 --> 00:25:20.679
Bryon Mackay: That we should also be thinking about the test just as much as we're thinking about the contact… the plan, the planning phase of… that we eventually hand to the AA agent.

160
00:25:20.830 --> 00:25:25.670
Bryon Mackay: It ensures not only that the, right

161
00:25:25.820 --> 00:25:31.500
Bryon Mackay: That the right functionality is there, but it also ensures that there's no regression later.

162
00:25:33.870 --> 00:25:44.339
Bryon Mackay: You may be doing this already, but something that I've almost forgotten to do, because I just have it… I have it drilled into our skill, is after every change, that it goes through and runs all the tests.

163
00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:50.540
Bryon Mackay: That it runs through them and checks for regression. And it's amazing, and kind of scary.

164
00:25:50.650 --> 00:25:56.149
Bryon Mackay: how often regression tests are failing because of something that the AI just wrote.

165
00:25:56.590 --> 00:26:09.489
Bryon Mackay: So, again, really important to have that set up, to have your tests set up. It's really important to make sure that your tests are covering the proper things, and the things that, are critical to your business.

166
00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:22.219
Bryon Mackay: Also important that you give the LLM, or I keep switching off between the two, but you know what I mean, the coding agent, the understanding and ability to go and run those tests every time.

167
00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:39.020
Bryon Mackay: So, I would just put a skill. Personally, what I do is I just… well, I put it in the… I think we have it in a skill, and we also have a CloudMD file, where we just say, after every change, when I say it's all done, like, always run the test before, you know, before committing.

168
00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:45.200
Bryon Mackay: And make sure that that's there. And of course, we have those things already also built in as pre-commit hooks.

169
00:26:45.770 --> 00:26:52.680
Bryon Mackay: But easier to have the LLM run it, and then go back and change it, and work through the problems themselves, and having to go back and forth.

170
00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:57.399
Bryon Mackay: That also is true for UI changes with, things like Playwright.

171
00:26:57.610 --> 00:27:05.149
Bryon Mackay: If you want to include that as well, it just is one less step that you have to do. Of course, now they all have their own… they're all coming out with their own,

172
00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:12.309
Bryon Mackay: Browser use so that you can go in there and… and simply say, hey, go spin up the app, go make sure it actually runs.

173
00:27:12.310 --> 00:27:30.110
Bryon Mackay: I would have loved to have had that, you know, a year ago, when I was doing some heavy UI changes to have it go figure that out, so I wouldn't have to be the one to do it, but… So anyway, a lot of great tools are coming out, and more still to come. Like I said, I still think we're in the early, early stages, and we haven't quite figured out where all this is going.

174
00:27:30.110 --> 00:27:32.739
Bryon Mackay: But when we do, it's gonna be… it's gonna be a heyday.

175
00:27:33.260 --> 00:27:35.480
Bryon Mackay: So,

176
00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:42.940
Bryon Mackay: Okay, I think we know this, we've been drilled with this in our heads as engineers for many, many years, so won't say any more on that.

177
00:27:44.070 --> 00:27:50.720
Bryon Mackay: Next, so we have, guard rails, are really…

178
00:27:51.210 --> 00:28:01.760
Bryon Mackay: it's… guardrails is such a funny term, I feel like, because it's… sometimes we say it to say, like, oh, you have to make sure your app has guardrails for all these things. I don't know, it just…

179
00:28:02.100 --> 00:28:10.029
Bryon Mackay: It's a very vague term in terms of what actually it is, but we're gonna… so we're gonna talk about what kind of guardrails you can put in place,

180
00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:17.670
Bryon Mackay: And we can… and then you can take that back to your work and see what, how you want to implement it.

181
00:28:18.260 --> 00:28:21.339
Bryon Mackay: But the first… is skills.

182
00:28:21.560 --> 00:28:31.759
Bryon Mackay: So, skills is an excellent, excellent way of defining a process that you want, the LLMs to do.

183
00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:46.210
Bryon Mackay: And they're super useful. So, for example, I, as part of my job, I pull down a lot of code, that I have no idea what's inside, right? I am pulling down code all the time and running it on my machine.

184
00:28:46.770 --> 00:28:55.139
Bryon Mackay: And so one thing I do is I have a set of skills that goes through the code and just makes sure that there's nothing nefarious in there, nothing that's gonna hose my machine.

185
00:28:55.600 --> 00:29:10.649
Bryon Mackay: And it's just… it's just part of the regular workflow. I've forgotten it's there, quite frankly. The only time I remember it's there is when I go, wow, this is taking a little bit longer than I thought, and then I look and it's, like, still looking through security concerns.

186
00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:22.609
Bryon Mackay: So… but it's great, because I never have to think about it again, and it just takes one more thing off my plate. So, if you have any of those, and it could be, let's see, I'll give you a couple examples here.

187
00:29:22.770 --> 00:29:36.350
Bryon Mackay: we had one that would check our Click House instance to make sure that things were… and then if there was an issue there, it would go and, update, or try to scale up our nodes, or scale down our nodes.

188
00:29:36.520 --> 00:29:50.960
Bryon Mackay: We had other ones that would do document generators, that was a really, really useful one, so whenever anything changed, we had a skill for that. But there's just all sorts of things where it's like, we always forget, right? So write a skill that just does it for you, and you don't have to remember as much, which is nice.

189
00:29:51.820 --> 00:29:53.500
Bryon Mackay: Okay.

190
00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:56.420
Bryon Mackay: So… Linters.

191
00:29:56.780 --> 00:30:16.070
Bryon Mackay: I still am a big advocate of lynching. It's static, yes. It's not AI, yes. But it's still really great, and it's a great thing for the LLMs, to be aware of. So, again, as part of your either pre-commit, or as part of your skills, or your CloudMD files, it's another great thing to put in there. It's like, hey, every now and then run this.

192
00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:22.030
Bryon Mackay: Make sure that, you know, when you change, when you're done changing, you know, 400 lines of code at a time.

193
00:30:22.350 --> 00:30:34.870
Bryon Mackay: go and run the linter and see where we're at. It'll catch a plethora of issues and mistakes well before, it hits production, and it just gives, again, the LLM a chance to self-correct.

194
00:30:35.360 --> 00:30:45.260
Bryon Mackay: So I'm still a huge, huge fan of lenders. I know there are… there's a lot of opinions out there about what lenders should do, but regardless, I think there's still a lot of value there.

195
00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:47.050
Bryon Mackay: Okay.

196
00:30:50.520 --> 00:31:02.439
Bryon Mackay: So, you can choose, as well, what models you're using for different tasks. Now, it's very easy for me to say, hey, I'm gonna use Opus, because Opus is the best, and it is.

197
00:31:02.590 --> 00:31:04.590
Bryon Mackay: Try to convince me otherwise.

198
00:31:04.860 --> 00:31:14.080
Bryon Mackay: But, sometimes we don't need it, right? However, when it comes to the critical gates that we have in our system.

199
00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:17.449
Bryon Mackay: That is where we want to use the best model possible.

200
00:31:17.590 --> 00:31:28.450
Bryon Mackay: Right? Before it goes out to production, when we have a coding agent go through and review our code, you better believe that we want the best thing possible, because we want to catch everything we can.

201
00:31:28.770 --> 00:31:29.810
Bryon Mackay: So…

202
00:31:30.110 --> 00:31:41.289
Bryon Mackay: Be mindful, right? We all have tokens, there's a cost, it's super easy for an LLM to become the most expensive thing in the engineering, budget.

203
00:31:41.770 --> 00:31:51.480
Bryon Mackay: But, so be mindful of that, but also when it comes to critical things, like, use the smartest model you can get your hands on for that verification.

204
00:31:51.830 --> 00:31:58.700
Bryon Mackay: Or, if you're using a lesser model, just make sure you give it enough context and enough guidance so that it can do a good job as well.

205
00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:02.320
Bryon Mackay: But I wouldn't leave that up to a weak one, and I think that's… I think that's pretty…

206
00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:04.680
Bryon Mackay: Pretty obvious, but worth saying.

207
00:32:06.390 --> 00:32:07.330
Bryon Mackay: Okay.

208
00:32:07.990 --> 00:32:17.219
Bryon Mackay: Finally, separate the loops. Keep the verification process distinct from the coding process. So,

209
00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:34.680
Bryon Mackay: this is… this is where you want to make sure that, you know, you still have other eyes on your code, right? You have other processes that are going through and verifying that, yes, what you have written is going to work in production. That can be QAing, that can be,

210
00:32:35.260 --> 00:32:40.580
Bryon Mackay: That can be, you know, other agents, that can be different… there's a whole lot of ways that we can go around this.

211
00:32:40.810 --> 00:32:47.119
Bryon Mackay: I would also want to say that verification does not happen always after the code's been pushed.

212
00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:50.870
Bryon Mackay: So, to give you an example.

213
00:32:50.990 --> 00:33:03.640
Bryon Mackay: We had, we had a case where we had to move data from a Postgres database to an OLAP database, because it was all analytical data that we wanted to aggregate. There's a lot of numbers that we wanted to crunch.

214
00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:08.460
Bryon Mackay: And, the verification process actually started in the planning phase.

215
00:33:08.760 --> 00:33:16.220
Bryon Mackay: So my co-worker, dutifully, did his research, went through the whole thing, and wrote this really great planning doc.

216
00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:33.689
Bryon Mackay: And it was my opportunity to then verify it, the plan. And so I went through it myself, I read it, I made some suggestions, most of it looked… it pretty much all looked great, there were just some additions I would have included, and then he went and built it, and it was… it was great. It was very, very smooth.

217
00:33:34.480 --> 00:33:42.429
Bryon Mackay: But again, we didn't rush it, right? He wrote it, and then I verified it, and then he wrote the code, and then I verified the code, and we had coding agents reviewing the code.

218
00:33:42.580 --> 00:33:48.540
Bryon Mackay: And it was a very, very smooth process. But you want to make sure that, of course, that you have

219
00:33:48.870 --> 00:33:53.500
Bryon Mackay: you know, multiple people look at that. If you've ever studied the, or looked into,

220
00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:59.199
Bryon Mackay: Oh, now I'm gonna forget the name. I wanna say Systems of Control, but I don't think that's quite the right term.

221
00:33:59.310 --> 00:34:13.539
Bryon Mackay: Where, basically, you can't… you don't give one person all the keys to handle finances, right? You have multiple people that have different keys, that have different gates that they're in charge of. Still want that, still need that. Does it slow things down? Sure. Sure, it does.

222
00:34:13.889 --> 00:34:19.359
Bryon Mackay: But we shouldn't let that speed be the determining factor on what we do.

223
00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:23.220
Bryon Mackay: Which leads me right to this thought.

224
00:34:23.540 --> 00:34:27.300
Bryon Mackay: If any of you know DEX, from Human Lair.

225
00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:35.659
Bryon Mackay: Segregation of duties. Jason. Yes, I think that's… I won't… I don't know if that's the exact right term, but that's really close to it.

226
00:34:36.429 --> 00:34:41.929
Bryon Mackay: It might even be the right one. It's been a while for me since I've had to use that term. Anyway…

227
00:34:42.889 --> 00:34:52.780
Bryon Mackay: This is from Dex, he's at Human Layer, you may know him, he's pretty… he's getting pretty big on… on X and other places. But he said this the other day, and I just… I think this is…

228
00:34:52.980 --> 00:34:56.710
Bryon Mackay: An excellent frame of mind to have as engineers.

229
00:34:56.870 --> 00:35:00.929
Bryon Mackay: He said the bottleneck is your ability to ensure quality.

230
00:35:01.970 --> 00:35:03.470
Bryon Mackay: I think that's so true.

231
00:35:03.870 --> 00:35:13.039
Bryon Mackay: I think that is absolutely what should be the bottleneck. I know we all want to push things fast, but for heaven's sakes, slow down.

232
00:35:13.190 --> 00:35:17.890
Bryon Mackay: And we should be pushing that up the leadership as well. That, look.

233
00:35:18.120 --> 00:35:22.960
Bryon Mackay: We're engineers, we're gonna make sure things work, we're gonna get more pushed out than ever before.

234
00:35:23.080 --> 00:35:26.520
Bryon Mackay: It may not just be the 100X that some people say.

235
00:35:26.710 --> 00:35:37.540
Bryon Mackay: It's not gonna be us open-clawing our way through, it's just not gonna work that way. And… if Anthropic's, little PR

236
00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:41.369
Bryon Mackay: Snafu that they had this last week.

237
00:35:41.500 --> 00:35:47.269
Bryon Mackay: Where we all got a peek a little bit into what Cloud Code actually looks like.

238
00:35:47.720 --> 00:35:54.789
Bryon Mackay: As well as that product works, there's some raised eyebrows. So, let's not be like that.

239
00:35:55.040 --> 00:36:04.520
Bryon Mackay: And let's make sure that our quality is good. It's really… it's almost becoming a bit of a moat, in a sense, these days. I don't know how long that will be.

240
00:36:04.730 --> 00:36:07.469
Bryon Mackay: But to have a quality application that's secure.

241
00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:15.409
Bryon Mackay: It's always been a mode, I suppose, but even more so today, with all the things that are going on, those metrics that we saw earlier kind of allude to that.

242
00:36:16.070 --> 00:36:22.790
Bryon Mackay: That we need to make sure that the quality of our code is still the most important thing that we can do with our jobs.

243
00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:28.400
Bryon Mackay: I do want to point out one other thing here, with this.

244
00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:30.490
Bryon Mackay: I saw an article the other day.

245
00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:39.590
Bryon Mackay: Excuse me. That I thought was actually… the headline was kind of funny, but also, said something about our jobs as engineers.

246
00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:50.339
Bryon Mackay: It said, CEO fires all lawyers and relies on ChatGPT for his lawsuit. Loses $250 million case.

247
00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:53.529
Bryon Mackay: And I just thought.

248
00:36:53.800 --> 00:37:01.359
Bryon Mackay: We as engineers are like those lawyers, and sometimes people feel like they are going to vibe their way out of a problem.

